## 3. Mutation (mine) — M11/M12/M13, promoting round-1 A1

Round 1 found two isolated survivors (a template literal, a `: string`
annotation) and called the gap advisory. I set out to measure the class
rather than the instances. Script kept at the mutation harness path below.

M11 — annotate EVERY usage constant with `: string` and drift it.
     27 constants annotated across 14 files.
     $ node --import tsx packages/commands/src/usage.test.ts .
       FAIL  scan found at least one per-command usage constant
       exit 1                                               CAUGHT
     The guard's only backstop fires when ALL constants vanish.

M12 — same, but leave pause.ts's two constants intact so `sawAny` holds.
     25 constants annotated and drifted
     ("bisellium " -> "bisellium-DRIFTED ", "--studio <dir>" -> "--STUDIO-DRIFTED <dir>")
     $ node --import tsx packages/commands/src/usage.test.ts .
       PASS  packages/commands/src/pause.ts:USAGE_PAUSE line in USAGE
       PASS  packages/commands/src/pause.ts:USAGE_RESUME line in USAGE
       PASS  scan found at least one per-command usage constant
       exit 0                                               SURVIVED
     30 checks became 2. 93% of the guard's coverage disappeared, silently.

M13 — the decisive one. Same, but also leave lifecycle.ts intact, because
     four pre-existing cli.test.ts assertions (ready/done/review/red) compare
     that file's usage text literally and would otherwise do the catching.
     21 constants annotated and drifted.
     $ npm test                exit 0 · 1080 PASS · 0 FAIL  SURVIVED
     $ npm run -s typecheck    exit 0                       (`: string` is valid TS)
     The whole regression suite is green. The PASS count silently drops
     1104 -> 1080: 24 assertions evaporated and nothing said so.

     And the drift is user-visible, not cosmetic:
     $ bisellium close
       usage: bisellium-DRIFTED close <opus-id> --STUDIO-DRIFTED <dir> [--repo <dir>]
     $ bisellium          (the banner, undrifted)
              bisellium close <opus-id> --studio <dir> [--repo <dir>]
     Per-command help and the banner now disagree — the exact divergence
     behaviour 5 exists to prevent — with npm test, typecheck and lint green.

THE PROPOSED FIX, EXECUTED:
     let sawAny = false        -> let seen = 0
     sawAny = true             -> seen++
     check("scan found at least one …", sawAny)
       -> check("scanned every known usage constant", seen === 30, `${seen}`)
     clean tree : PASS  scanned every known usage constant   30
     under M13  : FAIL  scanned every known usage constant    6   exit 1
     Three lines, no new file, catches the whole class. Confirmed by running it.

## 4. The four advisories — round-2 rulings

A1 (guard skips what it cannot parse) — PROMOTED TO BLOCKING. In THIS PR.
    New evidence moved it: round 1 had two isolated survivors, M13 has 21
    constants drifting user-visibly past a fully green suite. D-010 already
    decrees "a test that cannot fail is not evidence". Behaviour 5 IS this
    opus's deliverable; a guard whose coverage can fall from 30 to 2 without
    a word delivers the appearance of behaviour 5, not behaviour 5. That is
    the same defect this PR's own commit message indicts in the test it
    replaced ("its test passed because the fixture lex is ~200 tokens") —
    a check pinned to nothing. Shipping it and filing a follow-up opus would
    put the officina's oldest finding class back in the trunk in the PR
    written to close it. Three lines, already proven above.

A2 (undeclared paths) — ADVISORY, take it here, cheap.
    $ git diff --stat master...d3fec62 -- packages/
      packages/commands/src/index.ts | 1 +
    Confirmed in the diff, named in no list. briefs/W-030.md's "Files owned"
    bullets (lines 28-35) list eight paths; pause.ts / serve.ts / tick.ts are
    in the prose at lines 37-41, not the bullets. No `probatio.declared` rule
    exists yet, so nothing fails today — this is only cheap now and annoying
    later. Brief edit, no code, no gate re-run.

A3 (30 of 34 banner lines) — ADVISORY, brief text only. Measured:
    banner lines: 34
    commands with a backing constant (from the guard's own output): 30
    uncovered: check, init, context, query — exactly the four whose flags
    live in main.ts's FLAGS_BY_COMMAND. The brief's "Out of scope" (lines
    80-83) says "Reconciling USAGE against the parseFlags allowlists" but
    never says the consequence is 4 uncovered banner lines. One sentence.
    NOT blocking: it is an honest limit of a scoped feature, and unlike A1
    the covered 30 are genuinely covered.

A4 (behaviour 6's red covers 3 of 4) — ADVISORY, confirmed verbatim.
    studio/ci/reds/W-030/06.log, last four lines:
      FAIL  no args: prints banner, exits 2            status=2
      FAIL  unknown command: prints banner, exits 2    status=2
      PASS  unknown flag: prints banner, exits 2       status=2
      FAIL  --help: prints banner, exits 2             status=2
    Round 1's reading is exact. The behaviour as a whole went red so the red
    conforms to the W-020 contract; one of its four assertions has never been
    shown to fail. Not worth a re-record of an already-conforming red.

## 5. Merge preconditions that are NOT this opus's defect

D-015's named-and-unsolved gap is live on this PR and should be stated with
numbers before anyone merges, not discovered after:

  $ git merge-base --is-ancestor master d3fec62   -> NOT an ancestor
  $ git log --oneline d3fec62..master
      0819ea2 style: format scripts/gemini.mjs
      95a0045 docs: open W-032
  $ git diff --stat d3fec62...master -- . ':!studio' ':!examples'
      scripts/gemini.mjs | 5 ++++-

Master has moved under a SOURCE path since verify ran. tree:3df97d7e is the
branch's tree; it is not the tree that ships. D-015 records that
`probatio.certifies.stale` cannot catch this (scoped to ACTIVE opera, while
merge demands `done`) and that the question is open. Nothing in W-030 caused
this and W-030 should not be made to solve it — but the re-verify after
rebase is a manual step someone has to take, and this PR is where it bites.

