## 7. Verdict

FAIL — round 2.

Round 1's two blocking findings are fixed, and I proved it by recomputation
rather than by reading the front matter: the misfiled reds are gone from the
branch tree, and tree:3df97d7e is provably the branch's own source hash.

What fails is one thing I got wrong in round 1 and one thing the fix created:

  - A1 was not an advisory. 21 usage constants can drift user-visibly past a
    1080-PASS green suite (M13). That is D-010's "a test that cannot fail is
    not evidence", against this opus's own headline behaviour. Three lines,
    executed and confirmed, in this PR.
  - the branch now carries a blocking `traditio.present` on W-030 that master
    does not, it holds 4 of 5 declared gates, and its opus record conflicts
    with master's copy of the same file.

To pass:
  - pin the scanned-constant count in usage.test.ts (three lines; verified
    above to read 30 clean and FAIL at 6 under M13);
  - `bisellium handoff --opus W-030 --sella <id> --next <text>` so the branch
    stops adding a blocking finding to the trunk;
  - reconcile studio/opera/W-030.md with master's copy so the merge does not
    choose between the round-1 verdict and the four certificates;
  - (advisory) move pause/serve/tick into the brief's Files-owned bullets,
    add packages/commands/src/index.ts, and name the 4 uncovered banner
    lines in Out of scope.

Round 1 closed on "the code is fine and the evidence does not say what it
claims to say". Round 2 is narrower and sharper: the code is fine, the
evidence about the code is now true — and the test that is supposed to keep
it true can be made to check almost nothing without anyone noticing.
